<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qhapaq Ñan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Inca road system</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qhapaq Ñan was the road network of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, some 40,000 kilometres long, running along the Andes and the Pacific coast from present-day Colombia to Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Way stations called tambos and relay runners called chaskis allowed armies, officials and messages to move quickly. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan.jpg" alt="Qhapaq Ñan" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koen Adams · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inca_road_system.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
